## 6. The technology stack and why we chose it

### Languages

| Language | Where | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Dart** | The whole app (`taparide_flutter/lib`, about 16,000 lines) | Flutter's language: typed, fast, compiles to JavaScript/WebAssembly for the web and native code for Android |
| **SQL / PL/pgSQL** | Database schema, security rules and business actions (`taparide_flutter/supabase`) | Puts the rules next to the data, so every client obeys them, and makes each booking one atomic transaction |
| **JavaScript (Node.js, ES modules)** | Serverless API (`deploy/api`) and build/ops scripts (`deploy/scripts`) | Native on Vercel; keeps API keys on the server; quick to write |
| **JavaScript + three.js** | Tapi the 3D mascot (`taparide_flutter/web/mascot`) | Real-time 3D in the browser, embedded in the Flutter web app |
| **React (JSX)** | Marketing videos (`taparide_video`) with Remotion | Videos built as code: same brand, fonts and data as the app |
| **HTML / CSS** | Web shell and early prototype | Needed by every web app |

### Frameworks, services and libraries

| Piece | What we use it for | Why we chose it |
|---|---|---|
| **Flutter 3.47** | The app UI for web (desktop and mobile browsers) and Android | **One codebase, every screen.** Riders are on mid-range Android phones; judges and partners are on laptops. Flutter draws its own pixels, so our custom design looks the same everywhere. Strong typing and hot reload made a full product buildable by a small team in weeks |
| **go_router** | Navigation and role-based redirects | Deep links for every page (`/#/ride/r02`, `/#/portal/fcta`) and one place to send each role to its own home |
| **provider** | App state (`AppState`) | Simple, well known, enough for this size; no extra architecture to learn |
| **Supabase** | Sign-in (Auth), Postgres database, row-level security | A real Postgres database with built-in auth, a generous free tier, open source (no lock-in), and row-level security so permissions live in the database. It replaced writing our own backend server |
| **supabase_flutter** | Talks to Supabase from the app | Official client; handles sessions and calls the SQL functions |
| **Vercel** | Hosts the web app and runs the serverless API | Free static hosting plus serverless functions in one place, global CDN, edge caching for map tiles, encrypted ("sensitive") environment variables for keys |
| **Google Gemini** | Default AI: assistant, AI insights, document checks; text-to-speech voice for the commercial | Fast and affordable, handles images (documents) and tool calls. The newest "flash" model is picked automatically |
| **Anthropic Claude** (`@anthropic-ai/sdk`) | Second AI provider, used as fallback if a key is set | Strong tool use and reasoning; having two providers behind one interface means no single point of failure |
| **flutter_map + latlong2** | Route maps, live fleet map, demand map | Open-source maps with no per-load fee from Google |
| **CARTO basemaps** (via our `/api/tiles` proxy), **OpenStreetMap** fallback | Map tiles | Clean, light map style that suits the brand; the key stays on the server; edge cache keeps cost down; limited to the FCT region so the proxy can't be abused |
| **OpenStreetMap Nominatim** | Turning place names into coordinates (geocoding) | Free and good for Abuja place names; CARTO geocoding can be switched on later |
| **FindMe AI** (sister project) | Safety map, live traffic, road routes | Reused instead of rebuilt; reached through a `/api/findme` rewrite |
| **qr_flutter** | QR boarding passes and tap-card QR | Small and reliable |
| **shared_preferences** | Demo data, reminders and settings stored on the device | Works on web and Android with no setup |
| **geolocator, image_picker, url_launcher, http, crypto, web** | Location, document photos, links, network calls, password hashing, browser APIs | Standard, maintained Flutter packages |
| **Barlow + Fira Sans fonts** | Typography | Barlow fits a transport brand (condensed, legible on signage-like UI); Fira Sans supplies the ₦ sign, which Barlow lacks. Both are open-licence (SIL OFL) and self-hosted |
| **Remotion 4 + @remotion/three + React Three Fiber** | Ad, pitch film and narrated commercial | Videos are code, so every frame uses real brand tokens and can be re-rendered when the product changes |
| **Playwright** | End-to-end tests that click the real app like a user | Tests the live site on phone and desktop sizes |
| **flutter_test** | Unit, flow and permission tests | Built into Flutter |
| **pg** (node-postgres) | Database setup script | Runs migrations and seeds the live database through Supabase's connection pooler |
| **GitHub** | Source control | Team collaboration and history |

### Why this combination works

- **Small team, full product.** One app codebase (Flutter), one database that enforces its own rules (Supabase), and a handful of serverless functions (Vercel). No servers to manage.
- **Low running cost.** Every service has a free or low-cost tier that covers a pilot.
- **Secure by design.** Secret keys never reach the browser; permissions are enforced by the database.
- **Works offline for demos.** Without a database connection the app falls back to an on-device demo backend that applies the same rules, so a pitch never fails because of a network problem.
- **Portable.** Postgres, Flutter and Node are standard. We can move hosts without rewriting the product.

---

## 7. System architecture

```
                           ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  Rider phone /            │  Flutter app (web + Android)                 │
  partner laptop  ───────► │  lib/  · go_router · provider · flutter_map  │
                           │  Backend interface:                          │
                           │   ├─ SupabaseBackend  (live)                 │
                           │   └─ DemoBackend      (on-device fallback)   │
                           └────────┬──────────────────────┬──────────────┘
                                    │ Auth + SQL functions │ HTTPS /api/*
                                    ▼                      ▼
              ┌────────────────────────────┐   ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
              │ Supabase (Postgres)        │   │ Vercel serverless (Node.js)      │
              │ · Auth (email + password)  │   │ /api/assistant   → Gemini/Claude │
              │ · 11 tables, all with RLS  │   │ /api/insights    → Gemini/Claude │
              │ · security-definer actions │   │ /api/verify-documents → AI       │
              │   book_transit, top_up,    │   │ /api/geocode     → Nominatim     │
              │   review_partner_app, ...  │   │ /api/tiles       → CARTO/OSM     │
              │ · audit_log on every write │   │ /api/config      (flags only)    │
              └────────────────────────────┘   │ /api/findme/*    → FindMe AI     │
                                               └──────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Key design decisions**

- **One `Backend` interface, two implementations.** `lib/core/backend/backend.dart` defines everything the app needs. `SupabaseBackend` is the live one; `DemoBackend` keeps data on the device with the same rules. `main.dart` picks Supabase when the build includes a Supabase URL and key.
- **The database owns the rules.** Reads are filtered by row-level security; every write goes through a SQL function that re-checks the caller's role and writes the audit log.
- **The AI never computes numbers.** The app computes every figure from real platform data; the model only explains them. This keeps dashboards accurate.
- **AI tools run with the user's own permissions.** The assistant asks the app to run a tool (book a seat, list trips), and the app runs it as that user, so the AI can never do more than the user could.
- **Keys stay server-side.** The browser only ever gets the Supabase public (anon) key, which is designed to be public and is protected by row-level security.

