## 15. Business Model Canvas

| Block | Tap A Ride |
|---|---|
| **1. Customer segments** | Daily commuters in Abuja (students, civil servants, office workers, traders); transport operators (bus companies, shuttles, rail, EV companies); private drivers; fleet owners and vehicle investors; government (FCTA, DRTS, Transport Secretariat); employers with office clusters; later, state governments running CNG and electric bus fleets |
| **2. Value propositions** | **Riders:** know your fare, know your ride, just tap: fixed fares, published departures, verified vehicles, no cash, safer night travel, an AI guide. **Operators:** see every naira, fill empty seats, same-day settlement, demand data. **Fleet owners:** per-vehicle earnings that end under-remittance and make transport investable. **Government:** a verified-vehicle register against "one chance" crime, ridership and planning data, a ready system to run idle terminals. **Employers:** staff arrive on time and less stressed |
| **3. Channels** | Web app and Android app; onboarding desks at Berger, Area 1, Wuse and terminals; campus and office ambassadors; operator outreach and FCTA partnership; ATDEW demo and Investor Deal Room; social media ads and the commercial; travel-card agents |
| **4. Customer relationships** | Self-service app with an AI assistant; dedicated onboarding for operators and fleet owners; a partner portal per company; government partnership; community-based Tag Along circles; ratings and safety support |
| **5. Revenue streams** | Per-ride platform fee; fleet-owner subscription per vehicle; operator software plans; travel-card sales; Tag Along booking fee; data and planning contracts; employer plans (see section 16) |
| **6. Key resources** | The multi-tenant platform (app, database, AI layer); the verified-vehicle and partner register; ridership data; brand and the Tapi assistant; the team; relationships with operators and the FCTA; payment-partner integration |
| **7. Key activities** | Building and running the platform; onboarding and verifying operators, drivers and vehicles; rider acquisition; payment settlement; data analysis; customer support; regulatory engagement |
| **8. Key partners** | FCTA and DRTS; transport operators (Air Shuttle NG, Renewed Hope buses, Abuja Metro, private fleets); payment partners (Paystack or Flutterwave); a licensed Mobile Money Operator or bank (Phase 2 wallet); hardware suppliers (validators, DESFire cards); cloud providers (Supabase, Vercel, Google, Anthropic); FindMe AI; Young Innovators of Nigeria / ATDEW |
| **9. Cost structure** | Engineering and product team; cloud hosting, database and AI usage; payment-processing fees (borne in the split); validators and travel cards (bought after operators sign); field operations and onboarding desks; marketing; legal and compliance; customer support |

---

## 16. Business model and pricing

**We earn a small fee on every ride and a subscription from the people who manage vehicles. Riders never pay a platform charge on top of the fare.**

The concept brief lists the revenue streams but leaves prices to be set after the pilot. The prices below are **our planning assumptions**, chosen to be small next to typical Abuja fares (₦500–₦1,000 per trip). We will confirm them against operators' margins and riders' willingness to pay during the pilot.

| Revenue stream | Who pays | How | Planning price |
|---|---|---|---|
| Per-ride platform fee | Operators (inside the payment split) | Flat fee per paid ride, taken automatically | **₦50 per ride** (about 7–10% of a ₦500–₦700 fare) |
| Fleet-owner subscription | Fleet owners and investors | Monthly fee per vehicle: owner dashboard, reconciliation alerts, driver scorecards | **₦5,000 per vehicle per month** |
| Operator software plan | Larger operators | Advanced scheduling, analytics, several depots | **₦150,000 per month** |
| Travel card sales | Riders | One-off card fee covering the card's cost (low margin) | **₦1,000 per card** |
| Tag Along service fee | Riders | Small booking fee per seat, within the cost-sharing cap | **₦100 per seat** |
| Data and planning services | FCTA and agencies | Anonymised ridership and route reports under contract | **₦25M–₦60M per year** from Year 2 |
| Employer plans | Companies | Tag Along circles and commute subsidies for staff | **₦150,000 per company per month** |

### Unit economics of one ride (example)

| | Amount |
|---|---|
| Fare the rider pays (fixed, published) | ₦500 |
| Platform fee (ours) | ₦50 |
| Operator's share, before processing | ₦450 |
| Card processing (paid by the operator in the split; about 1.5% for local cards, confirm current Paystack pricing) | about ₦7.50 |

Cash collection today costs operators far more than this through under-remittance and lost fares, which is why the fee is an easy yes.

---

## 17. Revenue targets

These are **targets, not forecasts**. They rest on the assumptions shown and will be reset with real pilot data. Year 1 starts with the first paid pilot in December 2026.

### Volume assumptions

| Driver (yearly average) | Year 1 (Abuja pilot to launch) | Year 2 (Abuja at scale) | Year 3 (Abuja + 2nd city) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Paid rides per day | 6,000 | 40,000 | 120,000 |
| Fleet vehicles subscribed | 150 | 1,000 | 3,000 |
| Operators on a software plan | 3 | 10 | 25 |
| Travel cards sold in the year | 20,000 | 100,000 | 250,000 |
| Tag Along seats per day | 300 | 2,000 | 5,000 |
| Employers on a plan | 4 | 20 | 50 |
| Data contract | none | ₦25M | ₦60M |

For scale: Abuja has about 4.3 million residents making millions of trips a day, so 120,000 paid rides a day is only a few percent of the city's daily trips. Kaduna's 100 CNG buses alone carried about 3.2 million passengers in their first year.

### Revenue by stream (₦)

| Stream | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Per-ride fee (rides/day × 365 × ₦50) | 109,500,000 | 730,000,000 | 2,190,000,000 |
| Fleet subscriptions (vehicles × ₦5,000 × 12) | 9,000,000 | 60,000,000 | 180,000,000 |
| Operator plans (operators × ₦150,000 × 12) | 5,400,000 | 18,000,000 | 45,000,000 |
| Travel cards (cards × ₦1,000) | 20,000,000 | 100,000,000 | 250,000,000 |
| Tag Along (seats/day × 365 × ₦100) | 10,950,000 | 73,000,000 | 182,500,000 |
| Employer plans (companies × ₦150,000 × 12) | 7,200,000 | 36,000,000 | 90,000,000 |
| Data and planning contracts | 0 | 25,000,000 | 60,000,000 |
| **Total revenue target** | **₦162,050,000** | **₦1,042,000,000** | **₦2,997,500,000** |
| Recurring revenue (excluding card sales) | ₦142,050,000 | ₦942,000,000 | ₦2,747,500,000 |

**In short: about ₦162 million in Year 1, about ₦1.0 billion in Year 2 and about ₦3.0 billion in Year 3.**

### Fare value flowing through the platform

At an average fare of ₦600, the fares that pass through Tap A Ride (and settle straight to operators) would be about **₦1.3 billion in Year 1, ₦8.8 billion in Year 2 and ₦26.3 billion in Year 3.** The ₦50 fee is about 8% of that.

### Break-even target

If the Year 1 running cost is about **₦8 million a month** (a small team, cloud and AI, field onboarding desks), the non-ride income (fleet, operator, employer and Tag Along) covers about ₦2.7 million of it. The rest needs about **3,500 paid rides a day** at ₦50. That is the break-even target for the first year, below the Year 1 average of 6,000.

### Milestones that unlock the targets

1. **November 2026:** ATDEW live demo and National Innovation Challenge pitch; one operator signs a pilot.
2. **December 2026 – March 2027:** first live route; commuter survey; Paystack split payments live; first fleet owners on subscription.
3. **Mid 2027:** Air Shuttle NG or Renewed Hope buses on the platform; validators ordered; Tag Along launched with one office cluster.
4. **2028:** Abuja at scale; first FCTA data contract; Phase 2 wallet with a licensed partner.
5. **2029:** second city (Jos, Plateau is a natural candidate), then cities with large state fleets such as Kaduna and Enugu.

---

## 18. Market and competition

**Market**

- Nigeria's urban mobility and ride-hailing market is about **USD 400 million (2025)**, forecast to reach **USD 965 million by 2031** (Ken Research).
- Abuja is one of Africa's fastest-growing capitals: about 4.2–4.3 million people, growing about 4.6% a year (Macrotrends, UN data).
- Government is putting fleets on the road nationally: electric buses for civil servants, the electric airport shuttle, and the national CNG programme (Kaduna 100 CNG buses with about 3.2M passengers in year one; Plateau about 13,000 commuters a day; Enugu 100 CNG buses; Kano over 1,000 converted vehicles; Edo 50 CNG buses). Every one of them needs ticketing and revenue tracking.
- Lagos shows that digital fare collection works and lets revenue be audited (Cowry card).

**Competition: they run vehicles; we power everyone who does.**

| Player | Model | Our difference |
|---|---|---|
| Shuttlers | Runs its own buses on fixed routes; 430+ buses daily | We don't compete for seats; we power operators, government fleets and fleet owners. Shuttlers could even run on us |
| Treepz | Shared rides and ticketing | We focus on Abuja's public and private fleets, tap cards and owner transparency |
| Cowry card (Lagos) | State tap card for BRT, rail, ferries | Lagos only; we bring the model to Abuja, plus booking, Tag Along and fleet-owner tools |
| Bolt | Private car ride-hailing | We serve daily commuters at shared-transport prices; we deep-link to Bolt when a private car is needed. (Uber has left Nigeria) |

**Our moat:** a multi-tenant platform with the verified-vehicle register, per-vehicle money trail and government data. Every new operator makes the network more useful for riders, and every rider makes it more valuable for operators.

---

## 19. Go-to-market and scaling

| Group | Why they say yes | How we win them |
|---|---|---|
| Riders | Fixed fares, verified vehicles, no cash, less waiting | Free first travel card, launch discounts, onboarding desks at Berger, Area 1, Wuse and terminals, campus and office ambassadors |
| Operators | See every naira, fill seats, paid the same day | Free onboarding, no upfront cost, pay only per ride; phones as validators before hardware |
| Fleet owners | Stop under-remittance, prove returns | Dashboard trial on their vehicles; drivers who adopt get better terms |
| FCTA and DRTS | Run idle terminals, fight one-chance crime, get planning data | Offer the platform for the terminal PPP; share the register; pilot one corridor |
| Investors | A trustworthy, data-backed way into Abuja transport | ATDEW Investor Deal Room demo with live pilot numbers |
| Employers | Staff on time and less stressed | Tag Along circles for clusters like Aminu Kano Crescent |

**First pilot targets:** Air Shuttle NG (airport to Wuse 2), the Renewed Hope civil-servant electric buses, one private minibus or taxi fleet on Lugbe or Kubwa, and one office cluster for Tag Along.

**Scaling:** the platform is multi-tenant, so a new city is a new tenant, not a rebuild. Order: Abuja → a second city with strong ties (Jos) → states with large bus fleets (Kaduna, Enugu).

---

## 20. Risks and how we handle them

| Risk | How we handle it |
|---|---|
| Holding customer money without a licence | Phase 1: passes and pay-per-ride through Paystack or Flutterwave split payments; Phase 2: wallet held by a licensed MMO or bank |
| Tag Along seen as illegal taxis | Cost-sharing cap, verified professionals only, DRTS e-hailing route for drivers who want to earn, seek DRTS endorsement |
| Data protection | Comply with the Nigeria Data Protection Act; collect only what we need; row-level security |
| Low rider uptake | Awareness campaigns, onboarding desks, launch discounts, the AI assistant |
| Card fraud or cloning | DESFire cards, daily caps, instant block, rotating signed QR codes |
| Poor network on routes | Offline-first validators that sync later |
| Hardware delays | Phones as validators until a pilot is signed |
| Drivers still taking cash | Cheaper cashless fares and GPS-versus-revenue alerts |
| A public demo admin on the live database | Admin accounts are protected in the database; the master admin is private |
| Dependence on one AI or map provider | Two AI providers behind one interface; OSM fallback for maps; on-device answers for common questions |

This is not legal advice; positions must be confirmed with a Nigerian lawyer and, where needed, the CBN and DRTS.

---

## 21. What is real today and what is still simulated

**Real and working on the live site**

- The full app: rider, partner portal, FCTA, operator, fleet, driver and master admin dashboards
- Live Supabase database with sign-in, row-level security and all business actions
- Partner registration with AI document checks and admin review
- Tapi the 3D mascot and AI assistant (Gemini), AI insights, offline quick answers
- Real maps (CARTO through our proxy), geocoding, FindMe AI safety map and road routes
- Security hardening, encrypted keys on Vercel, live end-to-end tests
- Three marketing videos

**Simulated or still to build**

- **Payments are simulated.** The tap-card balance and top-ups are demo money. Next: Paystack split payments in test mode, then live.
- **No physical validators or cards yet.** The QR boarding pass works; the conductor validator app and NFC cards come with the pilot.
- **Dashboard figures are demo data**, labelled in the app, combined with baselines for the city. Real numbers start with the pilot.
- **Tag Along hosts accept automatically** after a few seconds; a host-side accept or decline flow is still to build.
- **Email confirmation** must be switched off in Supabase (or a mail sender configured) so that new sign-ups can log in.
- **Housekeeping:** pin the `@anthropic-ai/sdk` version in `deploy/package.json` (it currently says `latest`); remove the unused root-level Flutter starter and early prototype when convenient.

